react-hook-form keeps inputs uncontrolled precisely so typing does not go through React state. Calling `watch()` with no argument gives that benefit straight back: it sets the internal watch-all flag, so every field change notifies the form root and re-renders the component that owns all forty inputs. Scope is the real lesson, and the name of the API is not enough to tell you the cost: `watch('country')` flags only changes to `country`, so it re-renders the form root when the select changes but not on a keystroke in `email`, while `useWatch({ control, name: 'country' })` narrows the re-render to the component that calls it. The same discipline applies to validation state: read `formState.errors` in a small `<FieldError>` child rather than in the form root.

